      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_10",
      "quote": "The overall recurrence rates in the ESD and EMR groups were 0.62% and 12.02%, respectively, which aligns closely with the results of studies such as that by Russo et al. [21]. The higher recurrence rate in the EMR group observed in this meta-analysis is associated with both lesion size and the use of piecemeal resection [14, 22, 23]. This is further supported by the findings of Motomi Terasaki et al., who reported that the local recurrence rate for 40 mm LSTs was significantly higher than that for 20-39 mm lesions ( p < 0.001), and that lesions resected in three or more pieces had a notably higher recurrence rate compared with those resected in two pieces or en bloc ( p = 0.013). Similarly, in the study by Yutaka Saito et al., the recurrence rate was approximately 2% in the ESD group compared with approximately 14% in the EMR group, and recurrence after EMR occurred more frequently in piecemeal resection cases. Similarly, a systematic review and meta-analysis by Chandan et al. demonstrated that STSC after EMR for LSTs > 20 mm significantly lowered adenoma recurrence at the first surveillance colonoscopy (pooled rate: 6%; OR 0.27, p < 0.001) [26]. Similarly, a recent meta-analysis of randomized trials by Santo et al. confirmed that thermal ablation (including both STSC and APC) of post-EMR margins reduced recurrence rates (RR = 0.31, p < 0.001) without increasing the risk of adverse events or delayed bleeding [27].",
      "reason": "The discussion explicitly places the meta-analysis findings in context with other studies/meta-analyses (e.g., Russo et al., Motomi Terasaki et al., Yutaka Saito et al., Chandan et al., Santo et al.) and links observed effects to findings reported previously."
    }
  ],
